# type_overload.tkr — the §9 B type-overload-by-arity + `.{ … }` target-typed construction ACCEPTANCE channel.
# The type-decl key is `(namespace, name, ARITY)`, so `Pair`, `Pair<T>` and `Pair<T,U>` coexist in one
# namespace and resolve by the reference's SYNTACTIC arity; construction is ALWAYS a type
# prefix (`Pair { … }` / `Pair<i64> { … }`) or the target-typed `.{ … }`. Each scenario's probe answers a BOOL that
# only the correctly-selected declaration/construction makes true, announced by name. TEST = the
# printed token, never the exit code (the axis law).

Feature: §9 B type overload by generic arity + `.{ … }` target-typed construction

  # (arity_coexist) `Pair`, `Pair<T>` and `Pair<T,U>` coexist in one namespace and each construction
  # selects its own declaration by the reference's syntactic arity (rulings 1/3).
  Scenario: arity_coexist — three arities of one name coexist and resolve by syntactic arity
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario arity_coexist: ok"

  # (dot_static) a generic type's `static fn of` constructs the enclosing `Box<T>` via `.{ … }`,
  # phantom-stamped per width (§4.1) — the same path `List<T>::make` now takes.
  Scenario: dot_static — `.{ … }` in a static factory phantom-stamps per width
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_static: ok"

  # (dot_instance) an INSTANCE method constructs `.{ … }`, proving it is legal outside a static
  # factory (§4.1).
  Scenario: dot_instance — `.{ … }` is legal in an instance method
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_instance: ok"

  # (bare_arity0) a bare `Pair { … }` names the ARITY-0 declaration always, never borrowing the arity
  # of the coexisting `Pair<T>` (ruling 7).
  Scenario: bare_arity0 — a bare prefix constructs the arity-0 declaration
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario bare_arity0: ok"
